# .githooks/pre-push must execute as a Unix shell script on any
# platform that clones this repo -- pin it to LF regardless of the
# clone's core.autocrlf setting. A CRLF-corrupted hook can fail to
# execute in a way that's indistinguishable from the hook simply not
# being wired (see .githooks/pre-push's own comments and this repo's
# AGENT_RULES.md for why a silently-absent guardrail is a real risk,
# not a cosmetic one), found by an adversarial review, 2026-09-17.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning applies to any shell-script template in this kit --
# consuming projects copy these out as real, executed scripts, so a
# CRLF-corrupted template propagates the exact same silent-failure mode
# into every project that copies it. Two separate patterns, not one --
# `templates/pre-push-hook.template` has a `#!/bin/sh` shebang but no
# `.sh` extension, so `*.sh.template` alone doesn't match it. An
# adversarial review reproduced the gap directly: on a fresh Windows
# clone with the Git-for-Windows default `core.autocrlf=true`, this
# exact file checked out with a CRLF shebang (`#!/bin/sh\r\n`) -- the
# one template this whole file exists to protect, silently uncovered
# since this rule was first written, 2026-09-22.
#
# Adding an eol=lf rule to an already-tracked file does not, by itself,
# rewrite that file's line endings in an existing clone or index --
# git only applies the new attribute going forward. Run `git add
# --renormalize .` once after adding a rule like this (done here) so
# the fix actually takes effect on files git already knows about, not
# just newly-checked-out ones.
templates/*.sh.template text eol=lf
templates/pre-push-hook.template text eol=lf
templates/pre-commit-hook.template text eol=lf
templates/kit-go.sh.template text eol=lf
tests/kit-go/*.sh text eol=lf
templates/KIT-GO.md.template text eol=lf
templates/commands/*.md.template text eol=lf
KIT-VERSION text eol=lf

# Same reasoning again: these YAML files embed real shell scripts inside
# `run: |` blocks that a CI runner executes -- a CRLF-corrupted copy can
# leave a literal \r at the end of every embedded shell line, which
# bash treats as part of the command (a classic "$'\r': command not
# found"-shaped failure). Caught while fixing an unrelated YAML bug in
# governance-gate.yml.template on a Windows checkout, 2026-09-23 -- not
# yet reproduced as an actual CI failure, but the mechanism is identical
# to the already-confirmed .sh.template risk above, so pinned
# preemptively rather than waiting to reproduce it the hard way.
templates/workflows/*.yml.template text eol=lf

# License/attribution files: LF so any future content hash is stable across platforms.
LICENSE text eol=lf
NOTICE text eol=lf
PROVENANCE.md text eol=lf
release/*.sh text eol=lf

# Speed-mode templates: LF on every platform (a test asserts no CR bytes).
templates/SPEED-MODE.md.template text eol=lf
templates/AGENT-LEDGER.md.template text eol=lf
templates/SPEED-MODE-METRICS.md.template text eol=lf
REFERENCE-SETUP.md text eol=lf
